diff --git a/docs/next/website/src/content/docs/cli-reference.mdx b/docs/next/website/src/content/docs/cli-reference.mdx
index f2db44c94..34073fde4 100644
--- a/docs/next/website/src/content/docs/cli-reference.mdx
+++ b/docs/next/website/src/content/docs/cli-reference.mdx
@@ -447,8 +447,13 @@ herdr coordinator start [--repo DIR]
 herdr coordinator end [--reason TEXT] [--id COORDINATOR_ID]
 herdr coordinator status [--repo DIR]
 herdr coordinator handoff --to PANE [--id COORDINATOR_ID]
+herdr coordinator ready --note FILE
+herdr coordinator handoff --successor
+herdr coordinator ack --epoch N --snapshot SHA256
 ```
 
+A coordinator can hand its tenure to a fresh session of the same agent without a person in between. `coordinator ready --note FILE` freezes the caller's tenure: herdr copies the note into its state directory and records its sha256. While frozen, the tenure's mutations (`coordinator handoff --to`, `todo resume`, `todo run`, `worker start`, `worker answer`, `worker ack`) are refused with `coordinator_frozen`; reads, `worker prompt`, `worker stop`, `worker kill`, `worker interrupt`, `todo next` and `todo draft-run` are not fenced. `ready` is refused for an agent that is not a claude or pi session, or that is blocked on a question. `coordinator handoff --successor` writes herdr's own snapshot of the tenure (runs in flight with their last event ids, owned workers, pending question ids) and a versioned JSON manifest (epoch, snapshot and note paths with sha256, the old pane and the agent kind) to the state directory, opens a pane in the old pane's tab without moving focus and starts one fresh session of the same agent kind with a single launch prompt that names the manifest (`/todo --handoff PATH` for claude, `/skill:todo --handoff PATH` for pi). Any other agent kind is refused before anything starts. The successor reads the manifest and runs `coordinator ack --epoch N --snapshot SHA256` from its own pane; herdr then advances the epoch, moves the tenure, its workers and runs and the tab's coordinator role to the successor and closes the old pane, which ends the old agent session. A stale or wrong ack is refused (`handoff_refused`) and changes nothing. If the successor exits or reports a limit before its ack, the handoff aborts: the old tenure unfreezes and keeps its authority, and one notice says so. No timer is involved, and `coordinator status` shows a pending handoff's `state` (`frozen` or `successor_started`). A pending handoff lives in the server's memory, so a server restart unfreezes the tenure.
+
 `coordinator start` claims the coordination of the repository of `--repo` (default: the caller pane's directory) for the caller's pane (`HERDR_PANE_ID`): a tenure with an id of its own (`c-...`). It is refused with `coordinator_active`, naming the other tenure and its pane, while another pane coordinates that repository. `herdr tab role <tab> coordinator` does the same for the tab's focused pane, and the tab's `♛` follows the tenure: `coordinator end` (this pane's tenure, or `--id`'s) or `herdr tab role <tab> none` ends it, and so does closing its pane or exiting its agent. `coordinator status` lists the active tenures, each with the TODO item its `herdr todo run` works on until the run ends. Workers and runs started from a coordinator's pane carry its tenure's id (`owner_coordinator_id`) and belong to the tenure, not to the pane: when the coordinator's agent session comes back in another pane (`claude --resume`), the tenure, its workers, their obligations and its runs move there, and another session in the old pane inherits none of them. A `/clear` in the coordinator's pane keeps the tenure: the agent integration reports the new session (Claude's `SessionStart` with `source: clear`), and the tenure, with its workers and runs, goes on with it, so a later `claude --resume` of the new session finds it. `coordinator handoff --to PANE` passes the coordination on: this pane's tenure (or `--id`'s) ends `handed_off`, and the next one, one epoch on, starts in `PANE` with the item, workers and runs. The repository rooted at the home directory (a dotfiles repository) is claimed only explicitly, with `coordinator start --repo ~`: a pane whose directory is in it, by `coordinator start` without `--repo` or by the coordinator tab role, is refused, while a repository nested in the home directory is claimed as itself. Such a repository often keeps its git directory outside HOME (a bare repository that a `git` wrapper pairs with HOME), which herdr cannot find by looking for `.git`; then `coordinator start` failed with `invalid_request: <HOME> is not in a git repository`. With `--repo ~` the CLI asks git in HOME, with your shell's environment, for that directory (`git rev-parse --git-common-dir`) and sends it; the server checks that it is a repository's common directory that tracks files of HOME, records it in its state directory (`home-repositories.json`, so a restarted or handed-off server keeps it) and runs git there with `--git-dir` and `--work-tree`. Its folder slot lives in `~/.local/state/herdr/worktrees/home/<slot>` (or under `[workers] folder_slots_dir`), never beside HOME, and only once the repository ignores it; its workers write only that slot and their temp directory, never HOME itself or `~/.claude`.
 
 In a tab with the role `coordinator`, the Claude integration's `PreToolUse` hook holds the agent to what a coordinator does itself. It allows `herdr todo`, `worker`, `history`, `coordinator`, `report` and `reports`; `herdr agent read`, `list`, `get`, `explain`, `awaiting-reply` and `set-task`; `herdr-job wait`, `list`, `log`, `watch`, and `run` with an allowed command; read-only git (`status`, `log`, `diff`, `show`, `fetch`, `rev-parse`, `branch --list`, `ls-files`, without options that write a file or run a program); `git commit -- TODO.md DECISIONS.md` (by path, those files only); `python3 scripts/todo_edit.py`; `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `grep`, `rg`, `sed -n` (printing lines), `jq`, `ls`, `wc`, `date`, `df` and `du`; the consult helpers (`~/.claude/skills/*/ask_*`, `consult.py`); output redirected only to `/dev/null` or the session's scratchpad. Of the other tools it allows those that read or ask (`Read`, `Grep`, `Glob`, `AskUserQuestion`, `Skill`, `ToolSearch`, `TodoWrite`, and reading or stopping its own background shells), and `Write` and `Edit` only under the session's scratchpad (Claude's `<tmp>/claude-<uid>/<project>/<session>/scratchpad/`). Everything else is denied with the reason and the allowed path: builds, installs, pushes and cherry-picks happen in `herdr todo run`, code goes to a worker, `TODO.md` and `DECISIONS.md` are edited with `scripts/todo_edit.py`, work goes to a worker rather than a subagent. A compound command (`;`, `&&`, `||`, `|`, newlines, `$(...)`) runs only when every part is allowed, a quoted heredoc's body is data, and a command the hook cannot read for certain (backticks, subshells, brace or arithmetic expansion, a variable as the command or in a git argument, environment assignments, `&`) is denied. An exception is one Bash command that carries `# herdr-override: <reason>`: herdr records it (`coordinator.record_override`) and notifies you, and `herdr history overrides` lists it; when herdr cannot record it, the command is denied, so an exception is never silent. The hook asks herdr nothing for an allowed call; for any other it asks the tab's role, and outside a coordinator tab, or when herdr cannot tell, it does nothing.
